---
// /who-sees-what — the Art. 77(2) access lattice, operated rather than asserted.
//
// The point of this page is that a visitor changes the audience and watches
// fields appear and vanish. A table of the same information is a claim; a
// mechanism someone drives is an argument.
//
// SCOPE: batteries only, and the page says so in three places. ESPR Art. 11(b)
// defers access rights to each product group's delegated act, and none is
// adopted for the other eleven — so a general "authorities see less" claim
// would assert a rule that exists for one product group.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import access from "../data/access.json";

const title = "Who sees what — Art. 77(2) access, operable | Odal Node";
const description =
  "The battery passport's access rules are a lattice, not a ladder: an authority sees less than a recycler in one specific respect. Pick an audience and watch the fields change.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Section
    eyebrow="Access control"
    title="An authority sees less than a recycler."
    wide
  >
    <p>
      That sentence surprises people, so here is the mechanism rather than the
      claim. Pick an audience below and watch which parts of the record appear.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything on this page is <strong>Regulation (EU) 2023/1542 Art. 77(2)</strong> and{" "}
      <strong>Annex XIII</strong>, quoted from the consolidated text. It is
      the <em>battery</em> passport — see the note at the foot of the page for why
      that matters.
    </p>

    <div class="not-prose mt-10">
      <div role="group" aria-label="Choose an audience" class="flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        {access.audiences.map((a, i) => (
          <button
            type="button"
            data-audience={a.key}
            aria-pressed={i === 0 ? "true" : "false"}
            class="audience-btn rounded-lg border px-4 py-3 text-left transition focus:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2 focus-visible:ring-primary-500"
          >
            <span class="block text-sm font-semibold">{a.label}</span>
            <span class="block text-xs opacity-70">{a.sub}</span>
          </button>
        ))}
      </div>

      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-neutral-600">
        Basis: <span id="basis" class="font-mono">{access.audiences[0].basis}</span>
      </p>

      <ul class="mt-8 space-y-4">
        {access.points.map((p) => (
          <li
            data-point={p.id}
            class="point rounded-lg border border-neutral-200 p-5 transition"
          >
            <div class="flex items-start justify-between gap-4">
              <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-neutral-900">{p.title}</h3>
              <span class="state shrink-0 rounded px-2 py-1 text-xs font-semibold"></span>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-neutral-700">{p.note}</p>
            {p.only === "authority" && (
              <p class="mt-2 text-xs font-semibold text-primary-700">
                Authority only — not visible to a recycler.
              </p>
            )}
            {p.only === "legitimate" && (
              <p class="mt-2 text-xs font-semibold text-primary-700">
                Legitimate interest only — not visible to an authority.
              </p>
            )}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Why it is not a ladder" title="Neither audience contains the other." tone="muted">
    <p>
      Art. 77(2) assigns <strong>points 2 and 3</strong> to notified bodies,
      market surveillance authorities and the Commission, and{" "}
      <strong>points 2 and 4</strong> to holders of a legitimate interest.
    </p>
    <p>
      Point 3 is absent from the second list. Point 4 is absent from the first.
      No ordering can express that, so no integer "tier" can either — which is
      why an implementation that models access as a ladder with the regulator on
      top produces the wrong answer for point 4.
    </p>
    <p>
      Point 4 is the individual battery's own use history: charge cycles,
      accidents, temperature, state of charge. For an electric vehicle that is a
      usage record of an identifiable vehicle. Handing it to an audience the law
      does not grant it to is a personal-data disclosure, not a display bug.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Scope" title="This is the battery passport, and only the battery passport.">
    <p>
      ESPR does not impose this lattice. <strong>Art. 11(b)</strong> gives actors
      access <em>"based on their respective access rights set out in the
      applicable delegated act adopted pursuant to Article 4"</em> — so the split
      belongs to each product group's own act.
    </p>
    <p>
      For batteries that act exists and is quoted above. For textiles,
      electronics, furniture and the rest, <strong>no delegated act has been
      adopted</strong>, so there is no authority/legitimate-interest split to
      show. We model that as absence rather than guessing, and the product group
      manifests carry only the classes their own law defines.
    </p>
    <p>
      A fixed three-tier access model, applied everywhere, would be encoding one
      product group's rule as if it were the regulation's.
    </p>
  </Section>
</Base>

<script is:inline define:vars={{ audiences: access.audiences }}>
  (function () {
    const buttons = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(".audience-btn"));
    const points = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll(".point"));
    const basisEl = document.getElementById("basis");
    if (!buttons.length || !points.length) return;

    function apply(key) {
      const audience = audiences.find((a) => a.key === key) || audiences[0];
      const visible = new Set(audience.sees);

      for (const b of buttons) {
        const on = b.dataset.audience === audience.key;
        b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", on ? "true" : "false");
        b.classList.toggle("border-primary-500", on);
        b.classList.toggle("bg-primary-50", on);
        b.classList.toggle("border-neutral-200", !on);
      }

      if (basisEl) basisEl.textContent = audience.basis;

      for (const p of points) {
        const shown = visible.has(p.dataset.point);
        p.classList.toggle("opacity-40", !shown);
        p.classList.toggle("border-primary-300", shown);
        p.classList.toggle("border-neutral-200", !shown);
        const state = p.querySelector(".state");
        if (state) {
          state.textContent = shown ? "Visible" : "Withheld";
          state.className =
            "state shrink-0 rounded px-2 py-1 text-xs font-semibold " +
            (shown ? "bg-primary-100 text-primary-800" : "bg-neutral-100 text-neutral-500");
        }
      }
    }

    for (const b of buttons) {
      b.addEventListener("click", () => apply(b.dataset.audience));
    }
    apply(audiences[0].key);
  })();
</script>
